---

<LessonLayout
  title="L15 — Testing & Evaluation"
>
  <LessonHeader
    num="L15"
    title="Testing & Evaluation"
    subtitle="Three testing philosophies for agent systems, how testing tools differs from testing agents, guardrail testing, and eval pipelines against gold datasets."
  />

  <div class="lesson-content">

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Why testing agents is different</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional software tests are deterministic — same input, same output, every time.
      LLM outputs are probabilistic. The goal of agent testing is not to assert exact
      strings, but to verify <strong>structure</strong> (the right schema),
      <strong>behaviour</strong> (the right tools are called), and
      <strong>quality</strong> (the response is helpful and accurate).
    </p>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">§2.16 — Three Testing Philosophies</h2>

    <p>
      Agent systems contain three fundamentally different kinds of code that need
      three different testing approaches.
    </p>

    <div class="l15-philosophy-grid">
      <div class="l15-phil l15-phil--det">
        <div class="l15-phil-label">Deterministic</div>
        <p>Same input → same output, always. Tools, parsers, validators, prompt templates.</p>
        <p>Use exact assertions. No mocks needed. Fast. Run on every commit.</p>
        <code>expect(result.amount).toBe(49.00)</code>
      </div>
      <div class="l15-phil l15-phil--sto">
        <div class="l15-phil-label">Stochastic</div>
        <p>LLM output varies between calls. Can't assert exact text.</p>
        <p>Assert structure (JSON schema), presence (regex), routing behaviour (which tool was called). Use pass-rate thresholds, not 100% assertions.</p>
        <code>expect(chunk.type).toBe('text')</code>
      </div>
      <div class="l15-phil l15-phil--beh">
        <div class="l15-phil-label">Behavioural</div>
        <p>Test what the agent DOES, not what it says. Tool call sequences, state transitions, guardrail enforcement.</p>
        <p>Mock the executor. Assert chunk ordering and types. Test failure paths explicitly.</p>
        <code>expect(chunks[1].type).toBe('specialist_start')</code>
      </div>
    </div>

    <LessonCallout variant="key" title="Pick the right philosophy for each test">
      <p>
        Mixing philosophies produces brittle tests. Asserting exact LLM text (deterministic assumption)
        on a stochastic output will fail in CI randomly. Testing tool logic through the full agent
        stack (behavioural) is slow and obscures the failure. Match the approach to the code under test.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Testing Tools vs Testing Agents</h2>

    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Tool testing</th><th>Agent testing</th><th>Guardrail testing</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>What it covers</strong></td>
            <td>Pure tool logic, input validation, return shape</td>
            <td>Routing decisions, chunk ordering, executor calls, failure paths</td>
            <td>Injection rejection, PII rejection, input boundaries</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Output type</strong></td>
            <td>Deterministic — exact assertions</td>
            <td>Behavioural — type and ordering assertions</td>
            <td>Exception assertions — <code>rejects.toThrow</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>What to mock</strong></td>
            <td>Nothing — call the function directly</td>
            <td>The executor (<code>triageExecutor.run</code>, <code>reactExecutor.run</code>)</td>
            <td>Nothing — fire the command at the handler</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>LLM calls</strong></td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>None (executor mocked)</td>
            <td>None (rejected before LLM call)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Speed</strong></td>
            <td>Fastest</td>
            <td>Fast</td>
            <td>Fast</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>What it cannot catch</strong></td>
            <td>Wrong tool selected by LLM</td>
            <td>LLM output quality, hallucination</td>
            <td>Output-side PII leakage (needs output guardrail test)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="test-pyramid">
      <div class="test-layer test-l-eval">Eval pipelines — quality over a gold dataset (real LLM)</div>
      <div class="test-layer test-l-int">Integration tests — agent behaviour with mocked executor</div>
      <div class="test-layer test-l-unit">Unit tests — tools, parsers, prompt templates (no mock needed)</div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Tool Tests — Deterministic</h2>

    <p>
      Tools are pure functions. Call them directly with known inputs and assert exact outputs.
      No mocks, no async setup beyond the tool itself. These are the fastest tests and should
      run on every commit.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="issue-refund.tool.spec.ts" code={toolTestCode} />

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Agent Handler Tests — Behavioural</h2>

    <p>
      Mock the executor at the NestJS service level — not the HTTP layer, not the AI vendor SDK.
      The executor mock returns an async generator that yields controlled chunks. Assert chunk
      <em>ordering</em> and <em>types</em>, not exact text content.
    </p>

    <LessonCallout variant="tip" title="Mock the executor, not the HTTP layer">
      <p>
        Mocking at the HTTP layer (<code>msw</code>, <code>nock</code>) means your test
        depends on the exact wire format of the vendor SDK — a fragile coupling.
        Mocking the executor service means your test is independent of the vendor entirely.
        It also lets you return typed <code>AgentStreamChunk</code> objects rather than raw
        JSON blobs.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="ask-support-desk.handler.spec.ts" code={agentHandlerTestCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="warn" title="Always test the routing failure path">
      <p>
        Most agent test suites only test the happy path (triage selects a specialist, specialist
        responds). The failure path — triage produces no routing decision — is equally important
        and often skipped. If it's not tested, a silent routing failure will reach users as a
        blank response or uncaught exception.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Guardrail Tests — Boundary Assertions</h2>

    <p>
      Guardrails fire before the LLM is ever called. Test them in a separate describe block.
      The assertion is always <code>rejects.toThrow(BadRequestException)</code> — no mocking
      of executors needed because the request never gets that far.
    </p>

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="ask-support-desk.handler.validation.spec.ts" code={guardrailTestCode} />

    <LessonCallout variant="info" title="Test guardrails via the handler, not the schema directly">
      <p>
        Testing <code>AskSupportDeskCommandSchema.safeParse</code> directly verifies the schema
        but not that the decorator wires it up. Testing via <code>handler.execute</code> and
        <code>commandBus.execute</code> proves the full validation path is active end-to-end.
        Both matter — run both.
      </p>
    </LessonCallout>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">§4.3 — Eval Pipelines (Stochastic)</h2>
    <p>
      Eval pipelines run the agent against a curated gold dataset with <em>real</em> LLM calls
      and score each output. Run in CI on every prompt change to catch quality regressions
      before they reach production. These are slower and cost API budget — keep the gold
      dataset small and targeted (10–20 cases to start).
    </p>

    <LessonDiagram definition={`
flowchart LR
  D["Gold dataset\n(input + expected)"]:::data --> A["Agent\nunder test"]:::agent
  A --> S["Scorer\n(rules + LLM judge)"]:::score
  S --> R["Report\n(pass rate, scores)"]:::report
  R --> G{"Pass rate\n>= threshold?"}:::gate
  G -->|yes| P["Promote\nto prod"]:::ok
  G -->|no| F["Block\ndeploy"]:::fail

  classDef data   fill:#f8fafc,color:#475569,stroke:#e2e8f0
  classDef agent  fill:#dbeafe,color:#1e3a8a,stroke:#2563eb,stroke-width:2px
  classDef score  fill:#ede9fe,color:#4c1d95,stroke:#7c3aed,stroke-width:2px
  classDef report fill:#fefce8,color:#713f12,stroke:#fde68a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef gate   fill:#f8fafc,color:#64748b,stroke:#e2e8f0
  classDef ok     fill:#dcfce7,color:#14532d,stroke:#16a34a,stroke-width:2px
  classDef fail   fill:#fee2e2,color:#7f1d1d,stroke:#dc2626,stroke-width:2px
`} />

    <CodeBlock lang="typescript" label="eval-pipeline.ts" code={evalCode} />

    <h3 class="lesson-heading">Scoring methods</h3>
    <div class="lesson-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Method</th><th>Use when</th><th>Limitation</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Exact match</strong></td>
            <td>Structured outputs (JSON, classification labels, routing decisions)</td>
            <td>Too strict for free-text answers</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Contains / regex</strong></td>
            <td>Required keywords, format checks, prohibited content</td>
            <td>Doesn't capture meaning or quality</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>LLM-as-judge</strong></td>
            <td>Helpfulness, tone, factual accuracy in prose</td>
            <td>Adds cost and non-determinism; needs calibration</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Human eval</strong></td>
            <td>Final sign-off before major releases</td>
            <td>Slow and expensive — not for CI</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2 class="lesson-heading">Common Pitfalls</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Mocking at the HTTP layer instead of the executor level.</strong> Your test
        becomes coupled to the vendor SDK wire format and breaks on any SDK update. Mock the
        executor service — it returns typed <code>AgentStreamChunk</code> objects, not raw JSON.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Asserting exact LLM text in integration tests.</strong> The same prompt produces
        different wording each run. Assert chunk <em>types</em> and <em>ordering</em>, never
        exact strings. Save exact-match assertions for structured outputs like routing decisions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Testing only the happy path.</strong> The routing failure path — triage produces
        no routing decision — is just as likely in production and causes silent blank responses or
        uncaught exceptions. Always add a dedicated test for each failure mode.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Using <code>expect.anything()</code> for values that can be <code>undefined</code>.</strong>
        <code>expect.anything()</code> does not match <code>undefined</code>. A value like
        <code>traceId</code> from <code>AsyncLocalStorage</code> is <code>undefined</code> in
        tests — there is no <code>@TraceCommand</code> decorator setting the context. Always
        verify what a value actually is in the test environment before writing the assertion.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Guardrails tested incidentally, not explicitly.</strong> If your guardrail
        tests live inside the routing describe block, a failing routing test masks whether the
        fault is in routing or validation. Give injection detection, PII rejection, and schema
        validation their own describe block.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No test for the specialist failure path.</strong> In sequential fan-out, a
        specialist throwing prevents all subsequent specialists from running. That failure mode
        needs an explicit test — it will not surface in routing tests.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Never running evals after model version upgrades.</strong> A new model version
        can silently change routing preferences, tool-call patterns, and output style. Unit and
        integration tests will never catch it. Run the eval pipeline after every model swap.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Setting the eval pass-rate threshold too low.</strong> A 60% threshold lets
        significant regressions ship undetected. Start at 90% and tighten as the gold dataset
        matures and covers more edge cases.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <LessonChecklist
      title="What you can now do"
      items={[
        'Identify whether a piece of code needs deterministic, stochastic, or behavioural testing',
        'Write tool unit tests with exact assertions and no mocks',
        'Write agent handler tests by mocking the executor and asserting chunk ordering',
        'Write guardrail tests that prove injection and PII are rejected before the LLM is called',
        'Build an eval pipeline that scores LLM outputs against a gold dataset',
        'Use LLM-as-judge for quality dimensions that rules cannot capture',
        'Set a pass-rate threshold and block deploys that regress below it',
      ]}
    />

    <LessonQuiz questions={[
      {
        q:       'You need to test that your billing tool correctly rejects a refund when "confirmed" is false. Which testing philosophy applies?',
        options: [
          'Deterministic — call the tool directly and assert the exact result',
          'Stochastic — use a pass-rate threshold across many calls',
          'Behavioural — mock the executor and assert chunk ordering',
          'Eval pipeline — run against a gold dataset',
        ],
        answer:  0,
        explain: 'Tools are pure functions. Their output is deterministic — same input, same output every time. Call the tool directly and use exact assertions. No mocks, no LLM calls, no executors involved.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Your agent handler test uses expect.anything() for the traceId argument passed to the executor. The test passes locally but fails in CI. What is the most likely cause?',
        options: [
          'expect.anything() is not supported in Jest',
          'getTraceId() returns undefined when there is no async context, and expect.anything() does not match undefined',
          'The executor mock was not reset between tests',
          'CI runs tests in a different order',
        ],
        answer:  1,
        explain: 'expect.anything() matches any value that is NOT null or undefined. getTraceId() reads from AsyncLocalStorage context — in tests there is no TraceCommand decorator setting that context, so it returns undefined. The fix is to assert undefined explicitly in the test.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Why should guardrail tests be in a separate describe block from the agent routing tests?',
        options: [
          'Guardrail tests require a different testing framework',
          'Guardrails fire before any LLM call — they need no executor mocks and test a different boundary entirely',
          'Guardrail tests are slower and should run in a separate CI pipeline',
          'NestJS requires separate test files for validation and handler logic',
        ],
        answer:  1,
        explain: 'Guardrails (injection detection, PII, schema validation) fire before the executor is ever called. They need no executor mocks and test that bad input is rejected at the command schema level. Mixing them with routing tests obscures which boundary is under test and makes failures harder to diagnose.',
      },
      {
        q:       'Your eval pipeline tests quality of agent responses using an LLM-as-judge. The same agent output scores 4 in one run and 2 in the next. What is the most targeted fix?',
        options: [
          'Increase the gold dataset size',
          'Switch to human evaluation only',
          'Set the judge model temperature to 0 and require the judge to cite specific evidence before scoring',
          'Run the eval pipeline less frequently',
        ],
        answer:  2,
        explain: 'Inconsistent judge scores come from LLM output variance. Setting temperature=0 maximises determinism. Requiring chain-of-thought reasoning before the score forces the judge to commit to a rationale first, which further stabilises the score. These two changes address the root cause directly.',
      },
    ]} />

    <DeepDive
      sources="§2.16, §4.3"
      tensions={[
        { a: 'Mock executor for speed', b: 'Real LLM calls for confidence' },
        { a: 'Large gold dataset (thorough coverage)', b: 'Small curated dataset (fast CI feedback)' },
        { a: 'LLM judge accuracy', b: 'LLM judge cost and non-determinism' },
        { a: 'Test every guardrail pattern', b: 'Keep test suite fast' },
      ]}
      questions={[
        {
          q:    'Your support desk agent sometimes routes billing queries to the tech-support specialist — but only about 15% of the time. Your integration tests always pass because they use a deterministic mock. How do you surface this real routing problem?',
          hint: 'Think about what layer of testing can actually catch non-deterministic routing.',
          answer: '<p>Integration tests with a mocked executor are <strong>deterministic by design</strong> — they will never catch a 15% routing drift. The fix requires two things: (1) <strong>Eval pipeline with real LLM calls</strong> — run billing-intent inputs against the real supervisor and measure the routing accuracy across N runs (e.g. 10 calls per case). A 15% misroute rate shows up immediately. (2) <strong>Routing-specific gold cases</strong> — add cases like <code>"my invoice is wrong"</code> with <code>expectedSpecialist: "billing"</code> and a pass-rate threshold of ≥ 95%. This gates deploys when routing degrades. The integration test verifies <em>handler behaviour given a routing decision</em>. The eval verifies <em>the quality of the routing decision itself</em>. Both layers are needed.</p>',
        },
        {
          q:    'You have 133 tool unit tests passing and 16 agent handler tests passing. A production incident happens: the billing specialist runs get_invoice before the customer has provided an invoice ID, hallucinating one. None of your tests caught it. What test would have caught it, and why did it not exist?',
          hint: 'Think about what level of testing can verify the LLM follows the prompt rules.',
          answer: '<p>This is an <strong>eval-level failure</strong>, not a unit or handler failure. Your unit tests verify tool logic. Your handler tests verify routing and chunk ordering with a mocked executor — the mock returns whatever you tell it to, so a hallucinated tool call never appears. Only a test that runs the <em>real specialist against a real LLM</em> can catch prompt-following failures like this. The test that would have caught it: an eval case with input <code>"I have a billing problem"</code> (no invoice ID), with <code>forbiddenTools: ["get_invoice"]</code> until clarification is given, scored by checking that the agent asks a clarifying question first. This test did not exist because eval cases are typically built from happy-path inputs, not from the ambiguous inputs that cause real failures. Fix: mine support transcripts for cases where agents acted without enough information — those become your adversarial eval cases.</p>',
        },
        {
          q:    'Your LLM-as-judge scores are inconsistent — the same response gets a 4 in one run and a 2 in the next. How do you make judge scores stable?',
          hint: 'Think about what LLM parameters affect output variance, and what prompt techniques reduce it.',
          answer: '<p>Three techniques: (1) <strong>Set temperature=0</strong> on the judge model — maximises determinism (not perfectly deterministic but close). (2) <strong>Require chain-of-thought before the score</strong> — ask the judge to write its reasoning first, then output the score. The judge commits to a rationale before scoring, which anchors the number. (3) <strong>Multi-judge consensus</strong> — run 3 judge calls and take the median. Individual call variance averages out. Use a fast cheap model (Haiku) for judges to keep cost low even with 3 calls per case.</p>',
        },
      ]}
    />

  </div>
  <LessonMindMap content={mindMap} />

</LessonLayout>

<style>
